---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const compCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Lexing: characters → tokens', note: 'Split source text into words: identifiers, numbers, operators. "x = 3 + y" becomes a stream of five tokens.' },
    { math: '2 · Parsing: tokens → tree', note: 'Apply the grammar and build an abstract syntax tree. This is where "the semicolon is missing" errors are born — the parser is strict because ambiguity would be fatal.' },
    { math: '3 · Semantic analysis: does it mean anything?', note: 'Type-checking and scope resolution. The tree may parse; the compiler refuses "add a number to a date".' },
    { math: '4 · Optimisation: do less, same result', note: 'Dead-code removal, constant folding, loop unrolling — provably-equivalent rewrites. Modern compilers routinely outperform hand-tuning.' },
    { math: '5 · Code generation: tree → machine instructions', note: 'Emit assembly for the target CPU, link against libraries, produce the executable. Your idea has become voltage patterns.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/expert/compilers-and-languages">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>compiler translates human-writeable code into machine-executable
    instructions</strong> — and in passing, checks, restructures and improves it. The pipeline
    (lex → parse → analyse → optimise → generate) is one of the great engineering constructs:
    five well-defined stages that turned programming from punch-card craft into a global
    discipline.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five stages between your text file and running silicon" widget="equationStep" config={compCfg} />

  <h2>Why languages differ — and keep multiplying</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compiled vs interpreted:</strong> C compiles to machine code ahead of time (fast, strict); Python executes via an interpreter (flexible, slower). Just-in-time compilers (Java, JavaScript engines) compile <em>during</em> running — the hybrid that made the web fast.</li>
    <li><strong>Typing philosophies:</strong> static types (checked before running — C, Rust, TypeScript) catch errors early; dynamic types (checked while running — Python, JS) trade safety for speed of writing. TypeScript's rise shows the industry re-adding static types to dynamic languages.</li>
    <li><strong>Memory management:</strong> manual (C — fast, dangerous), garbage-collected (Java, Go — safe, with pauses), ownership (Rust — safe, fast, harder to learn). Each is a different answer to the same allocation problem.</li>
    <li><strong>Grammars are parseable:</strong> language design is constrained by what can be parsed efficiently — the reason your favourite language's syntax looks the way it does (mostly).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Aho, Lam, Sethi & Ullman, Compilers: Principles, Techniques and Tools ("the dragon book"); Backus, FORTRAN paper (1957).</p>
</TopicPage>
